          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em factorization lemma
            (
            %a{:href => "#Brown73"}> Brown 73
            , prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TheFactorizationLemma"}
            below) is a fundamental tool in the theory of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+fibrant+objects"} categories of fibrant objects
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+dual"}> dually
            \:
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibration+category"} of cofibrant objects
            %em factorization axioms
            in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            in that it asserts that every morphism may be factored as, in particular, a weak equivalence followed by a fibration.
          %p
            A key corollary of the factorization lemma is the statement, widely known as
            %em Ken Brown’s lemma
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#KenBrownLemma"}
            below) which says that for a functor from a category of fibrant objects to be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+functor"} homotopical functor
          %p
            For more background, see also at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P"} Introduction to classical homotopy theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Stable+homotopy+theory+--+P#FactorizationLemma"} this lemma
          %h2#factorization_lemma Factorization lemma
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            be a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p Let
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mi X
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mi Y
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X \overset{p_{X}}{\leftarrow} X \times Y \overset{p_{Y}}{\rightarrow} Y
            %p
              be a product in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_{X}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_{Y}
              are fibrations.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              By one of the axioms for a category of fibrant objects,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              has a final object
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            %p
              1) The following diagram in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              is a cartesian square.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi Y
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi p
                                  %mi Y
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi p
                              %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X \times Y               &amp;  \overset{p_{Y}}{\to} &amp; Y \\
                    p_{X} \downarrow &amp;                                      &amp; \downarrow  \\
                    X                            &amp; \to                                 &amp; 1 \\
                    }
            %p
              2) By one of the axioms for a category of fibrant objects, the arrows
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Y
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y \to 1
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \to 1
              are fibrations.
            %p
              By one of the axioms for a category of fibrant objects, it follows from 1) and 2) that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_{X}
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_{Y}
              are fibrations.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              be an object of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo ←
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 0
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mn 1
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    X \overset{p_{0}}{\leftarrow} X \times X \overset{p_{1}}{\rightarrow} X
            %p
              be a product in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi c
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi X
                              %mi I
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ↘
                              %mi Δ
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi e
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp;  \overset{c}{\to}                      &amp; X^I \\
                    \&amp;    \underset{\Delta}{\searrow} &amp; \downarrow e \\
                    \&amp;                                                   &amp; X \times X
                    }
            %p
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              in which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              is a weak equivalence, and in which
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              is a fibration.
            %p
              The arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_0 : X^I \to X
              given by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_0 \circ e
              is a trivial fibration. The arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mi I
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_1 : X^I \to X
              given by
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_1 \circ e
              is a trivial fibration.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p We have the following.
            %p
              1) The following diagram in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              commutes.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi c
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi X
                              %mi I
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ↘
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi id
                                  %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %msub
                              %mi e
                              %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp;  \overset{c}{\to}                                         &amp; X^I \\
                    \&amp;    \underset{id_X}{\searrow}                    &amp; \downarrow e_{0} \\
                    \&amp;                                                                    &amp; X
                    }
            %p
              2 By one of the axioms for a category of fibrant objects,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi id
                      %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id_X
              is a weak equivalence.
            %p
              By one of the axioms for a category of fibrant objects, we deduce from 1), 2), and the fact that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              is a weak equivalence, that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_{0}
              is a weak equivalence.
            %p
              An entirely analogous argument demonstrates that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi e
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_{1}
              is a weak equivalence.
          #TheFactorizationLemma.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (Factorization lemma)
            %p
              Let
