            %p
              The triangle on the right exists with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              a weak equivalence and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              a fibration, as categories of fibrant objects have path objects. As
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              is a fibration, we can pullback along
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo ×
                    %mi id
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \times id
              to get the upper-left pullback square. By the universal property of this pullback square, we can produce a unique
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi j
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \overset j \to Z
              which makes the top half of the diagram commute.
            %p
              We let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              be the composite
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                          %mi Z
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi Y
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z \overset{\pi_1^Z} \to X \times Y \overset{\pi_1^{X \times Y}} \to Y
              so that
              = succeed ";" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi g
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g \circ j = f
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e
              and a product projection (Fact 1), so their composite
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              is a fibration too.
            %p
              We let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              be the composite
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mn 1
                          %mi Z
                    %mi X
                    %mo ×
                    %mi Y
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mn 0
                          %mrow
                            %mi X
                            %mo ×
                            %mi Y
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z \overset{\pi_1^Z} \to X \times Y \overset{\pi_0^{X \times Y}} \to X
              so that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi r
                      %mo ∘
                      %mi j
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi id
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \circ j = id_X
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              is the pullback of the morphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Y
                      %mi I
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mi e
                    %mi Y
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi π
                          %mn 0
                          %mrow
                            %mi Y
                            %mo ×
                            %mi Y
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y^I \overset{e} \to Y \overset{\pi_0^{Y\times Y}} \to Y
              (which is an acyclic fibration by Fact 2), hence
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              is also an acyclic fibraiton.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              That
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              is a fibration can be demonstrated in exactly the same way as that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              is a fibration. It is to prove the stronger assertion that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              is a trivial fibration that the argument with which we concluded the proof is needed.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p By the commutativity of the diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi j
                          %mtd
                            %mi Z
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ↘
                              %mi id
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi r
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp;  \overset{j}{\to}                &amp; Z \\
                    \&amp;  \underset{id}{\searrow} &amp; \downarrow r \\
                    \&amp;                                           &amp; X
                    }
            %p
              and the fact that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              is a weak equivalence, we have, by one of the axioms for a category of fibrant objects, that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              is a weak equivalence.
          %h2#ken_browns_lemma Ken Brown’s lemma
          #KenBrownLemma.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p
              %strong (Ken Brown’s lemma)
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+fibrant+objects"} category of fibrant objects
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              be a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to D
              be a functor with the property that, for every arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              which is a trivial fibration, we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(f)
              is a weak equivalence.
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w : X \to Y
              be an arrow of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              which is a weak equivalence. Then
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi w
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(w)
              is a weak equivalence.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_4 Proof
            %p By Proposition 3, there is a commutative diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi j
                          %mtd
                            %mi Z
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ↘
                              %mi w
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi g
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp;  \overset{j}{\to}             &amp; Z \\
                    \&amp; \underset{w}{\searrow} &amp; \downarrow g \\
                    \&amp;                                        &amp; Y
                    }
            %p
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              such that the following hold.
            %p
              1) The arrow
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                    %mo :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g : Z \to Y
              is a fibration.
            %p
              2) There is a trivial fibration
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                    %mo :
                    %mi Z
                    %mo →
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r : Z \to X
              such that the following diagram in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              commutes.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi j
                          %mtd
                            %mi Z
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ↘
                              %mi id
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi r
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp;  \overset{j}{\to}                &amp; Z \\
                    \&amp;  \underset{id}{\searrow} &amp; \downarrow r \\
                    \&amp;                                           &amp; X
                    }
            %p By the commutativity of the diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi j
                          %mtd
                            %mi Z
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ↘
                              %mi w
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi g
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp;  \overset{j}{\to}             &amp; Z \\
                    \&amp; \underset{w}{\searrow} &amp; \downarrow g \\
                    \&amp;                                        &amp; Y
                    }
            %p
              and the fact that both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi w
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} w
              are weak equivalences, we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
              is a weak equivalence, by one of the axioms for a category of fibrant objects.
            %p
              By assumption, we thus have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(g) : F(Z) \to F(Y)
              is a weak equivalence.
            %p The following hold.
            %p 1) By the commutativity of the diagram
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mi j
                          %mtd
                            %mi Z
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ↘
                              %mi id
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi r
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X &amp;  \overset{j}{\to}                &amp; Z \\
                    \&amp;  \underset{id}{\searrow} &amp; \downarrow r \\
                    \&amp;                                           &amp; X
                    }
            %p
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒟
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{D}
              commutes.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi F
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi F
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi F
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ↘
                              %mi id
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi F
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi F
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    F(X) &amp;  \overset{F(j)}{\to}           &amp; F(Z) \\
                    \&amp;  \underset{id}{\searrow} &amp; \downarrow F(r) \\
                    \&amp;                                           &amp; F(X)
                    }
            %p
              2) Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
              is a trivial fibration, we have by assumption that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(r)
              is a trivial fibration. In particular,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(r)
              is a weak equivalence.
            %p
              3) By one of the axioms for a category with weak equivalences, we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi id
                    %mo :
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} id : F(X) \to F(X)
              is a weak equivalence.
            %p
              By 1), 2), 3) and one of the axioms for a category with weak equivalences, we have that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(j)
              is a weak equivalence.
            %p
              The following diagram in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
              commutes.
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi F
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi F
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi j
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi F
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Z
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo ↘
                              %mrow
                                %mi F
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi w
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi F
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi F
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    F(X) &amp;  \overset{F(j)}{\to}             &amp; F(Z) \\
                    \&amp; \underset{F(w)}{\searrow} &amp; \downarrow F(g) \\
                    \&amp;                                             &amp; F(Y)
                    }
            %p
              Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(j)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(g)
              are weak equivalences, we conclude, by one of the axioms for a category with weak equivalences, that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi w
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(w)
              is a weak equivalence.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              In other words,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+functor"} homotopical functor
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_4 Remark
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              is the full subcategory of fibrant objects in a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} right Quillen functor
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_5 Remark
            %p
              By the dual nature of
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} left Quillen functor
              preserves weak equivalences between cofibrant objects.
          %h2#computing_a_homotopy_pullback_by_means_of_an_ordinary_pullback Computing a homotopy pullback by means of an ordinary pullback
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                    %mo ←
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \to C \leftarrow B
              be a diagram between fibrant objects in a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %msubsup
                      %mo ×
                      %mi C
                      %mi h
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \times_C^h B
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                            %msubsup
                              %mo ×
                              %mi C
                              %mi h
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mi I
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi A
                            %mo ×
                            %mi B
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %mi C
                            %mo ×
                            %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    A \times_C^h B &amp;\to&amp; C^I
                    \\\
                    \\downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                    \\\
                    A \times B &amp;\to&amp; C \times C
                    }
            %p
              presents the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
              of the original diagram.
          %p
            See the section
            %em
              %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback#ConcreteConstructions"} Concrete constructions
            at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+pullback"} homotopy pullback
            for more details on this.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%88%9E-group"} ∞-group
              object in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              with
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo *
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} * \to \mathbf{B}G
              yields a morphism
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mo ≃
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi E
                      %mi G
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi p
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mi B
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} * \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} \mathbf{E}G \stackrel{p}{\to} \mathbf{B}G
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+principal+%E2%88%9E-bundle"} universal principal ∞-bundle
              for
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %h2#references References
          %p
            The
            %a{:href => "#TheFactorizationLemma"} factorization lemma
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#Brown73
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kenneth+Brown"}> Kenneth Brown
              , p. 421 (4 of 41) in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BrownAHT"} Abstract Homotopy Theory and Generalized Sheaf Cohomology
          %p
            The corollary commonly known as “
            %a{:href => "#KenBrownLemma"}> Ken Brown’s lemma
            ” does not appear explicitly in
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Brown73"} Brown 73
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mark+Hovey"}> Mark Hovey
              , Lemma 1.1.12 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Model Categories
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://www.math.rochester.edu/people/faculty/doug/otherpapers/hovey-model-cats.pdf"} pdf
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://books.google.co.uk/books?id=Kfs4uuiTXN0C&printsec=frontcover"} Google books
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/surv-63-s"} ISBN:978-0-8218-4361-1
          %p Lecture notes:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Homotopy+Theory#FactorizationLemma"} here
              and
              %a{:href => "Introduction+to+Homotopy+Theory#KenBrownLemma"} here
              in:
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Introduction+to+Homotopy+Theory"} Introduction to Homotopy Theory
          %p
            A version in the setup of
